            <ArticleTitle>The hunger genes: pathways to obesity.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>119-32</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.cell.2015.03.008</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0092-8674(15)00261-5</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The global rise in the prevalence of obesity and associated co-morbidities such as type 2 diabetes, cardiovascular disease, and cancer represents a major public health concern. The biological response to increased consumption of palatable foods or a reduction in energy expenditure is highly variable between individuals. A more detailed mechanistic understanding of the molecular, physiological, and behavioral pathways involved in the development of obesity in susceptible individuals is critical for identifying effective mechanism-based preventative and therapeutic interventions.</AbstractText>
